%erg 0.1
Title: Rebuild-binary CI must trigger on embedded asset changes
Created: 2026-05-29
Author: Claude

--- log ---
2026-05-29T19:04Z Claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`.github/workflows/rebuild-binary.yml` regenerates the committed bootstrap
binary `tickets/erg` on push to main. Its path filter watched only
`src/go/*.go` and `src/go/go.mod` — but the binary also embeds the
`src/go/assets/**` files via `//go:embed` (`bootstrap_assets.go`).

The 0160 fix (corrupted `≤` → `�i` in `assets/AGENTS.md`) was an asset-only
change. It did NOT match the path filter, so the rebuild never fired and the
committed binary kept shipping the corruption — i.e. `erg init` still unpacked
`random jitter �i 500ms`. The binary only got healed days later by accident,
when PR #172 touched `src/go/new.go` and triggered an incidental rebuild
(commit 26e66e2). Without that unrelated .go change, the corruption would have
shipped indefinitely.

The `tests/test_init.sh` pure-ASCII guard (added by 0160) correctly catches
the corruption — but only against a fresh `make build`, never against the
committed binary, by design (`_test-lint` forbids tests defaulting to the
committed binary). So nothing guarded the shipped artifact.

## Fix

Broaden the path filter to `src/go/**` so any input the binary is built from
(Go sources, go.mod/go.sum, embedded assets) triggers a rebuild. The existing
"Binary unchanged — no commit needed" guard makes no-op rebuilds (e.g.
test-only edits) harmless.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `rebuild-binary.yml` triggers on `src/go/assets/**` changes
- [ ] No-op rebuilds remain harmless (unchanged-binary guard still present)
